# Runtime state the services write beside themselves: the company record,
# the fleet room, the outbox, the read marks, and the session table.
#
# Matched by PREFIX, not by name. The named list that used to be here was
# defeated by an ordinary rename: HLL -> GMN moved every one of these to a
# gmn- name, none of which was listed, and the whole set — including 38
# live bearer tokens — was committed to a public repo before anyone
# noticed. A glob would have held through the rename. This one does.
# The trailing * matters as much as the leading one. These were listed as
# the bare names plus an explicit .tmp, which holds until somebody makes a
# copy: gmn-company.json.before-gmn001.bak matched none of them and sat in
# git status as untracked, one `git add -A` away from committing 77 real
# driver records and their email addresses. A backup of a secrets-bearing
# file is still a secrets-bearing file. Match anything derived from one.
hll-*.json*
hll-*.jsonl*
gmn-*.json*
gmn-*.jsonl*

# and any backup at all, whatever it was taken from
*.bak

# screenshots from tools/shots.js — generated, not source
.shots/


# .NET intermediates for the telemetry adapter. obj/ and bin/ are rebuilt by
# `dotnet build` every time and churn on every compile — two of them were
# already showing as modified with nobody having touched the source.
telemetry-adapter/obj/
telemetry-adapter/bin/
*.pdb

# The adapter itself. It used to be tracked on purpose, and that was the
# right call while it was 194 KB: telemetry-bridge.js spawns it at runtime,
# so a clone without it could not read the game.
#
# It is 65 MB now. Making it self-contained is what made it work on a machine
# with no .NET runtime — the framework-dependent build died on startup there,
# which the app then reported as "the adapter stopped on its own", forever.
# That is worth 65 MB in an installer. It is not worth 65 MB in git history
# on every adapter change, permanently, with GitHub warning past 50 MB and
# refusing past 100.
#
# Nothing breaks. CI only ever builds www/ and never touches the adapter, the
# installer carries it through build.extraResources, and a developer rebuilds
# it with `npm run telemetry:build` (needs the .NET 8 SDK). build-desktop.js
# checks for it first and says exactly that if it is missing.
gmn-telemetry-adapter.exe

.fake
telemetry-adapter/publish/

# Wrangler's local build/temp state for Cloudflare Pages. Regenerated on every
# `wrangler pages` run and specific to this machine.
.wrangler/

# Another generated payload, like www/ and app-www/ — built, never edited.
site/
